## 7. Database Design

Use Supabase PostgreSQL for persistent application data.

Create the required SQL migration files.

### Table: profiles

Suggested fields:
- `id`: UUID referencing `auth.users.id`.
- `username`: Unique normalized username.
- `email`: Email address.
- `full_name`: Full name.
- `course`: Degree or course.
- `branch`: Academic branch.
- `college`: College name.
- `semester`: Current semester or academic year.
- `graduation_year`: Nullable integer.
- `subjects_of_interest`: Array or JSONB.
- `preferred_language`: Optional text.
- `profile_completed`: Boolean.
- `created_at`: Timestamp.
- `updated_at`: Timestamp.

Use database constraints and indexes where appropriate.

### Table: conversations

Fields:
- Conversation ID.
- User ID.
- Conversation title.
- Created timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

### Table: messages

Fields:
- Message ID.
- Conversation ID.
- User ID.
- Role.
- Message content.
- Timestamp.

### Table: study_plans

Fields:
- Plan ID.
- User ID.
- Plan title.
- Subject.
- Start date.
- End date.
- Plan data.
- Creation timestamp.

### Table: study_tasks

Fields:
- Task ID.
- Plan ID.
- User ID.
- Task title.
- Description.
- Due date.
- Status.
- Updated timestamp.

### Table: quiz_attempts

Fields:
- Attempt ID.
- User ID.
- Subject.
- Topic.
- Questions and answers, when appropriate.
- Score.
- Attempt timestamp.

### Database Security

Enable Row-Level Security on every table containing user-specific information.

Users may access only records associated with their authenticated user ID.

Use `auth.uid()` in appropriate PostgreSQL policies.

Do not expose a Supabase service-role key to ordinary application sessions or frontend code. Prefer authenticated-user-scoped operations.

Use parameterized SQL where SQL is used. Do not delete or recreate tables on every application startup.

